NOTE
Nested arrays within collections are only partially supported in custom action request payloads.

You can pass a collection in custom action parameters that is dynamically populated at runtime.

Two types of collections are supported:

Simple collections
Use simple collections for lists of basic values, such as strings, numbers, or booleans. These are useful when you only need to pass a list of items without additional properties.

For example, a list of device types:
{
 "deviceTypes": [
     "android",
     "ios"
 ]
}

Object collections
Use object collections when each item includes multiple fields or properties. These are typically used to pass structured data, such as product details, event records, or item attributes.

For example:
{
"products":[
   {
      "id":"productA",
      "name":"A",
      "price":20.1
   },
   {
      "id":"productB",
      "name":"B",
      "price":10.0
   },
   {
      "id":"productC",
      "name":"C",
      "price":5.99
   }
 ]
}

NOTE: Nested arrays within collections are only partially supported in custom action request payloads.

String Functions

Learn how to use String functions in the personalization editor.

The document provides a comprehensive library of string manipulation functions that can be used in personalization contexts, including:

- camelCase: Capitalizes the first letter of each word in a string
  Syntax: {%= camelCase(string)%}
  Example: {%= camelCase(profile.homeAddress.street) %}

- contains: Determines if a string contains a specified substring
  Syntax: {%= contains(STRING_1, STRING_2, CASE_SENSITIVE) %}
  Example: {%= contains(profile.person.name.firstName, "A", false) %}

- concat: Combines two strings into one
  Syntax: {%= concat(string,string) %}
  Example: {%= concat(profile.homeAddress.city,profile.homeAddress.country) %}

These functions provide powerful tools for manipulating and checking string values in personalization scenarios, allowing for complex conditional rendering and text transformations.

